<body>
<h1>Sample size planning with samplesizeSimulation</h1>
<p>Louis Chen</p>
<p>This package evaluates prediction models across simulated sample sizes.
The result is an exploratory learning curve under a specified data generating
mechanism. It does not estimate formal statistical power.</p>
<!--begin.rcode setup, include=FALSE
library(samplesizeSimulation)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", cache = FALSE)
end.rcode-->
<h2>A small reproducible simulation</h2>
<p>These examples use deliberately few repetitions to keep checks fast.
For substantive studies, increase repetitions and examine Monte Carlo stability.</p>
<!--begin.rcode simulation
result <- run_general_simulation(
  sample_sizes = c(30, 60, 90),
  n_simulations = 3,
  target_models = "lm",
  metrics = c("mse", "r_squared"),
  infinite_n = NULL,
  cv_folds = 3,
  cv_repeats = 1,
  seed = 123,
  verbose = FALSE
)
summary(result)
result$results$lm$mse
end.rcode-->
<h2>Interpret the recommendation</h2>
<p>Candidate sizes count total generated observations. Cross-validation trains
on fewer observations. Error-metric efficiency measures the fraction of the
observed improvement from the smallest to largest candidate size. For metrics
where larger is better, efficiency is the ratio to performance at the largest
size. Values are clipped to [0, 1]. A 0.95 threshold is therefore a relative
performance convention; it does not mean 95% confidence, 95% accuracy, or
95% statistical power.</p>
<p>The argument <code>infinite_n</code> adds a finite reference simulation. It
cannot establish a known infinite-sample limit. Recommendations depend on the
candidate grid, reference run, model, metric, effect assumptions, and validation
design. A deteriorating error reference, a missing endpoint, or a nonpositive
higher-is-better reference produces a missing recommendation. Equal endpoint
errors use efficiency one by convention and deserve inspection.</p>
<p>The columns <code>q025</code> and <code>q975</code> summarize variability
across simulations; they are not confidence limits for the mean. Inspect
<code>n_success</code> and <code>n_failed</code> before relying on an average.</p>
<h2>Reuse simulations</h2>
<!--begin.rcode cache
cached <- run_general_simulation(
  sample_sizes = c(30, 60, 90),
  n_simulations = 3,
  target_models = "lm",
  metrics = c("mse", "r_squared"),
  infinite_n = NULL,
  cv_folds = 3,
  cv_repeats = 1,
  seed = 123,
  verbose = FALSE,
  lookup_table = result
)
cached$cache
recommend_from_simulation_table(cached$simulation_table)
end.rcode-->
<p>Tables reuse exact configuration keys with matching model, metric, and sample
size. Updated methods invalidate earlier cache keys. Keys do not certify
compatibility across R or dependency versions; retain session information and
regenerate tables after changing the modeling environment.</p>
<h2>Evaluate existing ordered observations</h2>
<p>Use original observations for a sequential prediction task. The ordered
evaluator trains on earlier blocks and evaluates subsequent blocks.</p>
<!--begin.rcode ordered
observations <- data.frame(
  time = seq_len(60),
  x = sin(seq_len(60) / 5),
  y = sin(seq_len(60) / 5) + cos(seq_len(60) / 3)
)
fit_and_evaluate_model(
  data = observations,
  model_type = "lm",
  response_var = "y",
  predictor_vars = "x",
  metrics = "mse",
  cv_folds = 3,
  cv_repeats = 1,
  validation_strategy = "ordered",
  order_var = "time"
)
end.rcode-->
<p>This example illustrates the interface, not a validated forecasting system.
The evaluator does not implement gaps, purging of overlapping outcome periods,
grouped folds, or automatic refitting of feature preprocessing inside folds.
Ordered validation is disallowed with bootstrap or synthetic resampling in the
simulation function. Random bootstrap folds can share duplicate original
records and do not establish independent external performance.</p>
<h2>Models and metrics</h2>
<!--begin.rcode catalog
get_model_registry(available_only = TRUE)
calculate_metrics(c(0, 0, 1, 1), c(0.2, 0.1, 0.8, 0.9),
                  c("auc", "accuracy", "brier"))
end.rcode-->
<p>Optional learners require their listed packages. Classification metrics
require 0/1 outcomes; AUC is missing when only one class occurs. Ordinal metrics
score numeric category ranks. Mixed targets require a <code>.group</code> column
and fit random intercepts with population-level predictions. Parametric
categorical predictors use numeric scores; empirical predictors can be factors.
Adjusted R-squared and a standalone efficiency metric are not supported.</p>
<h2>Practical limits</h2>
<p>The package operates on tabular predictors. Image models require an embedding
or learner adapter and independent image/group splits. Financial applications
require original chronological data, market-regime validation, and execution
costs in an external backtest. Relative sample-size convergence alone cannot
justify a trade. Parallel execution is not implemented. Results are written
only when an output directory is explicitly supplied.</p>
</body>
</html>
